using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

using FerriteLib.UiKit.Kernel;

namespace UniversalSqueaker;

/// <summary>
/// US's surface tokens, resolved from ONE style document instead of a C# assignment list (redesign S2),
/// and since the WO-US-UI-REFACTOR round from ONE PALETTE TABLE: the document is generated by
/// <see cref="UsSwatches"/> from the six official role tables, and the active swatch is a persisted
/// setting (default Core).
///
/// <para>
/// <b>US owns the COMPLETE baseline (R2).</b> The table starts from <see cref="UiTheme.Vanilla"/> and
/// declares <see cref="ColorTokens"/> in full — every token, including <c>AccentGold</c> and the four
/// per-surface edges — so US's look is not a partial overlay on a carrier template the library is free
/// to retire. After <see cref="Configure"/>, no token on the bag still carries a vanilla value; the
/// swatch lanes check the document/list agreement and the resolved values for EVERY swatch, not just
/// the default.
/// </para>
///
/// <para>
/// <b>Missing colour vs explicitly transparent (R2 item 4).</b> A token the document does not mention
/// keeps the baseline's value (the fail-soft path); a token declared as <c>#RRGGBB00</c> is a real,
/// authored answer that resolves to a fully transparent colour. Both spellings are reachable and the
/// lane asserts the distinction on a real document.
/// </para>
///
/// <para>
/// <b>A swatch is pure colour.</b> The generated document declares colours only — no font, no density,
/// no metric. Font and density are the two LAYOUT-BEARING theme axes (moving either advances
/// <see cref="UiTheme.LayoutRevision"/>), and they live in the layout manifest's own
/// <c>&lt;Styles&gt;</c> section plus the carrier. Selecting a swatch therefore applies no layout
/// default: <see cref="ApplyToLive"/> mutates an already-running host's theme bag through the carrier's
/// own resolver, which advances <see cref="UiTheme.ColourRevision"/> and drops the cached region clones
/// while leaving the arranged page, the band cache, the scroll positions and the focus exactly where
/// they were. The historical per-region colour schemes in the manifest are retired with this: the
/// swatch's own role values (WorkspacePlane, PanelBorder, the per-surface edges) are what spell the
/// regions now, which is the only way one switch can repaint the whole page.
/// </para>
///
/// <para>
/// There is still no attention token here on purpose: <c>UiTheme.Warning</c> is the carrier's redirect
/// onto <c>Danger</c>, so US carries the attention role in <see cref="UsAttention"/> instead - one hue
/// that no swatch touches. The official input records it as a supplemental semantic role: the attention
/// brush and its dark ink live in <see cref="UsAttention"/> alone, spelled nowhere else, and it is NOT
/// a new FL colour token. <c>SelectedBorder</c> is deliberately NOT declared by any swatch either: its
/// fallback (<c>SelectedBorder ?? AccentGold</c>) IS the current selection-edge contract.
/// </para>
///
/// <para>
/// Public because the kernel-host harness references the built mod assembly rather than linking this
/// source; internal would make the table unobservable from the only place that can assert it.
/// </para>
/// </summary>
public static class UsTheme
{
    /// <summary>
    /// The shipped default swatch's identity accent, spelled the way the table spells it
    /// (<c>#B49C73</c>, the Core Bronze/Lark family member). Named separately because the surface lane
    /// pins the accent against THIS constant rather than against a carrier swatch. Every swatch owns
    /// its own accent through the token; this constant documents the default identity.
    /// </summary>
    public const string AccentGoldHex = "#B49C73";

    /// <summary>
    /// The colour tokens every official swatch is required to declare. This is the "US owns the
    /// complete baseline" rule made checkable: the document generator walks this list, so a token
    /// dropped from the contract is a lane failure instead of an inherited carrier value nobody chose.
    /// <para>
    /// <c>SelectedBorder</c> is absent ON PURPOSE and that absence is the one documented exception: it
    /// must stay unclaimed so the accent fallback (<c>SelectedBorder ?? AccentGold</c>) survives.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    public static readonly IReadOnlyList<string> ColorTokens = new[]
    {
        "Base", "WorkspacePlane", "Panel", "SectionBand", "Raised", "Hover", "Selected",
        "Success", "Danger",
        "Border", "BorderStrong", "Divider",
        "BaseBorder", "PanelBorder", "RaisedBorder", "HoverBorder",
        "TextPrimary", "TextSecondary", "TextDisabled", "TextOnGold", "TextOnDanger",
        "DangerBorder",
        "AccentGold", "SwitchThumbOff"
    };

    /// <summary>The persisted swatch in force (fail-soft: null settings or an unknown id resolve to
    /// Core). This is the ONE read every surface takes; the writing side goes through the settings
    /// facade so persistence and the live repaint cannot disagree.</summary>
    public static string ActiveSwatchId
    {
        get
        {
            string id = UniversalSqueakerMod.Settings?.uiSwatchId ?? "";
            return UsSwatches.IsKnown(id) ? id : UsSwatches.DefaultId;
        }
    }

    // --- the one shared chrome bag (settings window title/close, dev panel, confirm dialog) ---
    /// <summary>
    /// The process's ONE shared chrome theme bag. The window SHELL chrome (the settings window's own
    /// title bar and close affordance, the dev panel, the confirm dialog) draws from a bag that is
    /// separate from any page host's bag: the page bag is repainted on a swatch switch by
    /// <see cref="ApplyToLive"/>, and this bag is repainted by <see cref="ReapplyChromeSwatch"/>. It
    /// is shared rather than per-window on purpose - the swatch is a page-wide decision, these bags
    /// carry no per-widget mutation (only the swatch writer touches them), so one bag cannot let one
    /// window "paint another" the way two sharing HOST bags would. A fresh Surface() still hands each
    /// HOST its own bag (the resolver mutates those); a window chrome that wants to FOLLOW a live
    /// switch reads this one.
    /// </summary>
    private static UiTheme? chromeTheme;

    public static UiTheme ChromeTheme
    {
        get
        {
            UiTheme bag = chromeTheme ??= Configure(UiTheme.Vanilla, ActiveSwatchId);
            return bag;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Repaint the shared chrome bag to the CURRENTLY ACTIVE swatch. The swatch write path
    /// (the source facade) calls this right after it repaints the attached page host's own bag, so a
    /// single switch lands on the page and the chrome together - colours only, no host or window
    /// rebuild. Reading the live ActiveSwatchId (not a passed id) keeps the two repaints from ever
    /// naming different palettes.</summary>
    public static void ReapplyChromeSwatch()
    {
        UiTheme bag = chromeTheme ??= Configure(UiTheme.Vanilla, ActiveSwatchId);
        ApplyToLive(bag, ActiveSwatchId);
    }

    /// <summary>The active swatch's resolved entry (never null, never unknown).</summary>
    public static UsSwatch ActiveSwatch => UsSwatches.Resolve(ActiveSwatchId);

    /// <summary>
    /// The DEFAULT swatch's style document text — the surface the swatch lanes read out and compare
    /// the resolved values against. Generated, so it cannot drift from the table.
    /// </summary>
    public static string SchemeXml => UsSwatches.DocumentFor(UsSwatches.Resolve(UsSwatches.DefaultId));

    /// <summary>
    /// A fresh instance, one per window that needs one — the same contract as the carrier's template
    /// (which hands out a new bag per call). A theme is mutated at runtime (a host applies a style
    /// document's page level into it, and a swatch switch re-tints in place), so two windows sharing
    /// one bag would paint each other. The bag is tinted with the ACTIVE swatch.
    /// </summary>
    public static UiTheme Surface()
    {
        return Configure(UiTheme.Vanilla, ActiveSwatchId);
    }

    /// <summary>Applies the ACTIVE swatch to <paramref name="theme"/> in place (fail-soft contract as
    /// before). Retained for the historical call shape; every surface already passes an id.</summary>
    public static UiTheme Configure(UiTheme theme)
    {
        return Configure(theme, ActiveSwatchId);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Applies <paramref name="swatchId"/>'s colours to <paramref name="theme"/> in place through the
    /// carrier's resolver and returns it. Fail-soft on purpose: a malformed generated document must not
    /// take the mod down with a <c>TypeInitializationException</c>, so the surfaces fall back to
    /// <see cref="UiTheme.Vanilla"/> and the failure is one loud line plus an entry on
    /// <see cref="SchemeIssues"/>. The swatch lanes are the positive control — they assert the
    /// resolved token values of EVERY swatch, so a document that stopped parsing reddens there instead
    /// of shipping the wrong look.
    /// </summary>
    public static UiTheme Configure(UiTheme theme, string swatchId)
    {
        if (theme == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(theme));
        new UiStyleResolver(theme, UsSwatches.ParsedDocument(swatchId)).ApplyTo(theme);
        return theme;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The live switch: re-tint a theme bag that is ALREADY painted by a running host. The carrier's
    /// resolver writes the colour tokens onto the injected bag, which advances
    /// <see cref="UiTheme.ColourRevision"/> (the clock that drops the resolver's cached region clones)
    /// and leaves <see cref="UiTheme.LayoutRevision"/> exactly where it was (the clock the engine's
    /// band cache compares). Callers pair this with a content-revision bump so the next pass re-reads
    /// the bindings; nothing else about the session is touched — no host rebuild, no new session, no
    /// scroll or focus change. This is the only colour-switch mechanism US uses.
    /// </summary>
    public static void ApplyToLive(UiTheme theme, string swatchId)
    {
        if (theme == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(theme));
        Configure(theme, swatchId);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Everything wrong with any parsed swatch document, discovered when it is read: the carrier
    /// parser's own drops (unknown token, unreadable colour, duplicate). US adds nothing to them.
    /// A lane asserts the list is empty, which is what keeps "a dropped declaration is never silent"
    /// a checkable fact for every swatch in the table.
    /// <para>
    /// DECLARED BEFORE <see cref="Scheme"/> ON PURPOSE: static field initializers run in textual
    /// order and <see cref="LoadScheme"/> writes into this list, so a later declaration would hand
    /// it a null list and the type initializer would fail before any surface was built - the
    /// fail-soft error path would become a hard crash. The original R2 file carried this ordering
    /// note; the palette rewrite restored the declarations but not the order, and the
    /// CORRECTIONS-R1 pass puts the guarantee back where the guard comment can point at it.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private static readonly List<string> schemeIssues = new();

    /// <summary>
    /// The parsed DEFAULT swatch document, or the empty document when generation/parsing failed.
    /// Public for the same reason as before: a swatch-only invalidation is a page-level re-apply of
    /// exactly this document through <see cref="UiHost.StyleResolver"/>, and this is its parse.
    /// </summary>
    public static UiStyleDocument Scheme { get; } = LoadScheme();

    public static IReadOnlyList<string> SchemeIssues => schemeIssues;

    private static UiStyleDocument LoadScheme()
    {
        try
        {
            UiStyleDocument document = UiStyleDocument.Parse(SchemeXml);
            for (int i = 0; i < document.Issues.Count; i++)
            {
                schemeIssues.Add("parser: " + document.Issues[i].Message);
            }

            return document;
        }
        catch (Exception error)
        {
            Verse.Log.Error("[US] the generated us-root style document did not parse; surfaces fall back to the"
                + " carrier's Vanilla template: " + error.Message);
            schemeIssues.Add("parse failure: " + error.Message);
            return UiStyleDocument.Empty;
        }
    }
}
